---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Cite from '../components/content/Cite.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/content/Placeholder.astro';
import FigureStrip from '../components/content/FigureStrip.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import StatusPill from '../components/content/StatusPill.astro';
import ScreeningPathway from '../components/science/ScreeningPathway.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'global-burden', title: 'The global burden' },
  { id: 'kazakhstan', title: 'Lung cancer in Kazakhstan' },
  { id: 'why-stage-matters', title: 'Why stage at diagnosis matters' },
  { id: 'why-screening-is-hard-to-reach', title: 'Why screening is hard to reach' },
  { id: 'what-would-have-to-be-true', title: 'What would have to be true' },
];

const refs = ['ji2025', 'akhmedullin2024', 'avizova2026', 'nlst2011', 'dekoning2020', 'wilson1968'];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="problem"
  toc={toc}
  refs={refs}
  brief={[
    'Lung cancer is the most common cancer and the leading cause of cancer death worldwide.',
    'In Kazakhstan it caused 16% of cancer deaths between 2014 and 2022, and most patients are diagnosed at an advanced stage.',
    'CT screening saves lives in trials, but it depends on scanners, specialists and follow-up care that are unevenly spread.',
  ]}
>
  <Section id="global-burden" title="The global burden">
    <p>
      In 2022 an estimated 2.48 million people were diagnosed with lung cancer and 1.82 million died from it. That makes
      it both the most frequently diagnosed cancer and the leading cause of cancer death, in countries at every level of
      development.<Cite id="ji2025" />
    </p>
    <FigureStrip
      items={[
        { value: '2.48', unit: 'million', label: 'new lung cancer cases worldwide in 2022', refId: 'ji2025' },
        { value: '1.82', unit: 'million', label: 'deaths from lung cancer worldwide in 2022', refId: 'ji2025' },
        { value: '18.7', unit: '%', label: 'of all cancer deaths, about one in five', refId: 'ji2025' },
      ]}
    />
  </Section>

  <Section id="kazakhstan" title="Lung cancer in Kazakhstan">
    <p>
      A nationwide analysis of 123,622 cancer deaths recorded in Kazakhstan between 2014 and 2022 found that cancers of
      the trachea, bronchus and lung accounted for 16.01% of them, the largest share of any cancer site in the
      study.<Cite id="akhmedullin2024" />
    </p>
    <p>
      The picture at diagnosis is just as important. In a national cohort of 13,402 lung cancer patients diagnosed from
      2019 to 2023, stage III was the most common stage recorded (47.5%), and median overall survival was 167 days.
      Three out of four patients died during follow-up.<Cite id="avizova2026" />
    </p>
    <FigureStrip
      items={[
        { value: '16.01', unit: '%', label: 'of cancer deaths in Kazakhstan, 2014–2022, were lung cancers', refId: 'akhmedullin2024' },
        { value: '47.5', unit: '%', label: 'of patients in a national 2019–2023 cohort were recorded at stage III', refId: 'avizova2026' },
        { value: '167', unit: 'days', label: 'median overall survival in the same cohort', refId: 'avizova2026' },
      ]}
    />
    <Placeholder title="Regional access to lung cancer screening and diagnosis in Kazakhstan" where="src/pages/problem.astro">
      <ul>
        <li>Current national screening policy for lung cancer, if any, with an official source.</li>
        <li>Where CT scanners and thoracic oncology services are located, and travel distances for rural residents.</li>
        <li>What clinicians interviewed by the team said about late presentation.</li>
      </ul>
    </Placeholder>
  </Section>

  <Section id="why-stage-matters" title="Why stage at diagnosis matters">
    <p>
      The strongest evidence that finding lung cancer earlier saves lives comes from randomised screening trials. In the
      US National Lung Screening Trial, 53,454 people at high risk were screened with either low-dose CT or chest X-ray.
      Low-dose CT reduced deaths from lung cancer by 20%.<Cite id="nlst2011" /> The European NELSON trial also found lower
      lung cancer mortality with CT screening.<Cite id="dekoning2020" />
    </p>
    <Note kind="context">
      <p>
        These trials screened people selected for high risk, mostly by age and smoking history. The benefit applies to
        that kind of programme, not to testing everyone.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="why-screening-is-hard-to-reach" title="Why screening is hard to reach">
    <p>
      A screening programme is a chain of steps, and each step depends on people and equipment being available where the
      patient lives. CT screening also produces many positive results that turn out not to be cancer: in the NLST, 96.4%
      of positive low-dose CT screens were false positives, each of which needed follow-up.<Cite id="nlst2011" />
    </p>

    <ScreeningPathway
      label="A lung cancer screening pathway, with the proposed breath-based triage step"
      steps={[
        {
          title: 'Identify people at high risk',
          detail: 'Usually by age and smoking history.',
          needs: 'Primary care records and outreach',
        },
        {
          title: 'Breath-based triage',
          detail: 'A low-cost first test that could help prioritise who is sent for CT.',
          needs: 'A validated test, which does not exist yet',
          proposed: true,
        },
        {
          title: 'Low-dose CT scan',
          detail: 'The screening test proven in randomised trials.',
          needs: 'CT scanners and trained radiologists',
        },
        {
          title: 'Diagnostic work-up',
          detail: 'Further imaging and biopsy for suspicious findings.',
          needs: 'Specialist centres',
        },
        {
          title: 'Treatment',
          detail: 'Earlier stage usually means more treatment options.',
          needs: 'Oncology services',
        },
      ]}
    />

    <p>
      OncoNose does not try to replace CT. The question is whether a cheap, portable first step could help health
      systems decide who most needs a scan, especially where scanners are far away. That is a hypothesis, and
      the <a href={href('safety-and-security')}>limitations</a> are serious.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="what-would-have-to-be-true" title="What would have to be true">
    <p>
      In 1968, Wilson and Jungner set out principles for when screening for a disease is justified, and they are still
      the starting point for screening decisions.<Cite id="wilson1968" /> We use a selection of them as a checklist for
      OncoNose, and we are honest that most answers are still open.
    </p>

    <Table label="Screening principles applied to OncoNose">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Principle</th>
            <th scope="col">For lung cancer</th>
            <th scope="col">For a breath-based test like OncoNose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">The condition is an important health problem</th>
            <td><StatusPill kind="met" /></td>
            <td>Applies to any lung cancer screening, including ours.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">There is a recognisable early stage</th>
            <td><StatusPill kind="met" /></td>
            <td>Early-stage disease exists; whether breath patterns change at that stage is an <StatusPill kind="open" label="open question" />.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">There is a suitable test</th>
            <td><StatusPill kind="met" label="Low-dose CT" /></td>
            <td><StatusPill kind="unmet" /> No validated biological breath test exists. This is what the project investigates.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">The test is acceptable to the population</th>
            <td><StatusPill kind="open" /></td>
            <td>Breathing into a device is non-invasive. Acceptability still has to be asked, not assumed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Facilities exist for diagnosis and treatment</th>
            <td><StatusPill kind="open" /></td>
            <td>A triage test is only useful if referrals lead somewhere. See <a href={href('human-practices')}>Human practices</a>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>
